    {
      "role": "user",
      "content": "that makes sense. so it's basically just a cycle of them eating different things and helping each other out. but what actually controls the balance between the two? like if my starter is way too sour or not sour enough, is that because one group is outperforming the other?",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "The balance between your yeast and your bacteria is absolutely controlled by the \"feeding schedule,\" which is basically a strict diet plan for your little microbial colony. If your starter is too sour, it means your *Lactobacillus* gang has been given too much sugar to eat without enough yeast to clean up the mess, so they've gone into overdrive and flooded the dough with acid. Conversely, if it's not sour enough, the yeast is winning the race to the sugar, eating it all up before the bacteria can get a bite, leaving the dough bubbly but bland.\n\nThink of it like a two-team tug-of-war where the rope is your dough's flavor profile. If you feed the starter too frequently, you're basically overfeeding the bacteria, who are the ones who love the leftover scraps and love to make the environment acidic. If you let it go too long without feeding, the yeast gets stressed and might die off, or worse, wild molds could sneak in and start a riot. The key is to feed them at the right ratio—usually equal parts flour and water—to keep both teams happy and balanced.\n\nIt's also worth noting that temperature plays a huge role here; a warm kitchen is basically a party",
      "source": "target",
